Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Flakhound**, a four-legged anti-air walker that strides
on legs, sweeps a back turret onto a bearing, and elevates twin flak barrels to track
the sky — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build
it by **painting discrete voxel cells** — one recorded operation at a time — and you
**author its walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Flakhound is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you
break the walker into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, target-tracking
anti-air platform needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

`voxel-anim` paints **discrete opaque cells**: you set and clear single voxels, fill and
stroke boxes, draw 3D lines and spheres, and mirror across a plane — each voxel an
opaque `#rrggbb` color, with no transparency. Every operation targets a **part** with
`--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. The volume starts **empty**. Each
call only records the operation to that part's log and renders **nothing** — run
`voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled scene (`--component
<part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at
an instant of an animation), and you **must** run `voxel-anim render` before you
finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the run is scored from — an
unrendered part is scored as empty.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 1 --y0 1 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 4 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim fill-box --part turret --x0 2 --y0 3 --z0 2 --x1 4 --y1 5 --z1 4 --color "#565c64"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their keyframes), and
`voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the
authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's
progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`,
`scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole walker composed from
all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm
the parts actually fit together and that the legs reach the ground. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished walker; the scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the walker up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly,
and the rig is complete: a squat armored body carried on legs, a turret up on its back,
and twin flak barrels raked toward the sky. **Author the two required animations** as
F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: `walk` (a walk whose feet plant
flat and still, then lift and swing — with weight, not a weightless slide) and
`flak_track` (the turret traversing and the barrels elevating to track a target while
the walker stands its ground). The animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by
those names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. When you are satisfied,
stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
